{"name":"Fog of War","hex":"#112233","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fog of war","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The uncertainty in situational awareness experienced by participants in military operations.","The shrouded concealment of enemy activity outside of areas observable by a player's forces."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fog_of_war"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fog of war","blocks":[{"text":"The fog of war is the uncertainty in situational awareness experienced by participants in military operations. The term seeks to capture the uncertainty regarding one's own capability, adversary capability, and adversary intent during an engagement, operation, or campaign. Military forces try to reduce the fog of war through military intelligence and friendly force tracking systems."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fog_of_war"},{"title":"Fog","blocks":[{"text":"Fog is a visible aerosol consisting of tiny water droplets or ice crystals held in the air near the Earth's surface. Fog can be considered a type of low-lying cloud usually resembling stratus and is heavily influenced by nearby bodies of water, topography, and wind conditions. In turn, fog affects many human activities, such as shipping, travel, and warfare. Recent atmospheric research indicates that fog also functions as an active aquatic ecosystem. Fog droplets harbor microbiomes where bacteria actively grow, divide, and interact with atmospheric chemistry."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/A_foggy_winter_morning.jpg/330px-A_foggy_winter_morning.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fog"},{"title":"War","blocks":[{"text":"War is an armed conflict between the armed forces of states, or between governmental forces and armed groups that are organized under a certain command structure and have the capacity to sustain military operations, or between such organized groups."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Stele_of_Vultures_detail_01a.jpg/330px-Stele_of_Vultures_detail_01a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Richard_III_from_Columbia_Games.jpg/500px-Richard_III_from_Columbia_Games.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Richard_III_from_Columbia_Games.jpg/1280px-Richard_III_from_Columbia_Games.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Richard_III_from_Columbia_Games.jpg/500px-Richard_III_from_Columbia_Games.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A block wargame, Richard III by Columbia Games, showing the fog of war in play: the red player can see the identity of their own pieces, but not those of the white player.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARichard_III_from_Columbia_Games.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Freeciv-net-screenshot-2011-06-23.png/500px-Freeciv-net-screenshot-2011-06-23.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Freeciv-net-screenshot-2011-06-23.png/1280px-Freeciv-net-screenshot-2011-06-23.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Freeciv-net-screenshot-2011-06-23.png/500px-Freeciv-net-screenshot-2011-06-23.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In the computer game Freeciv, completely unexplored areas are fully black, while explored but currently unobserved areas are covered in a grey shroud.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFreeciv-net-screenshot-2011-06-23.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/FogParticles.jpg/500px-FogParticles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/FogParticles.jpg/500px-FogParticles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Minute droplets of water constitute this after-dark radiation fog, with an ambient temperature of −2 °C (28 °F). Their motion trails are captured as streaks.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFogParticles.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/FogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg/500px-FogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/FogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg/500px-FogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A close-up view of water droplets forming fog. Those outside the camera lens's depth of field appear as orbs.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFogParticlesHighSpeed.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg/500px-Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg/1280px-Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg/500px-Frankfurt%2C_Friedrich-Ebert-Anlage_in_the_fog.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":